preConditionHook:
// GRIMES'S NAMED "BREAKOUTS, ENTERING IN THE PRECEDING BASE" TEMPLATE
// (G06-51), carried as the book states it and with its parts INLINED, as the
// corpus does for a named setup (FinalFlagReversalBreakoutEntry is the
// precedent). The LONG anticipates an UPSIDE break of resistance and is taken
// INSIDE the base, before the break -- functionally a support-holding trade,
// which is why it is not in the BreakoutPullback* family.
//
// FOUR PARTS, ANDed, the second an OR:
//   1. CLEAN LEVEL TOUCH BAND (G06-41 = LevelTouchCount with its maxTouches
//      amendment): the level has been tested at least minTouches and at most
//      maxTouches times and held every time -- "tested cleanly multiple times",
//      not worn out.
//   2. A BASE PRESSED AGAINST IT: a tight base near the level (G06-42 =
//      BreakoutBase) OR higher lows running into a flat ceiling (G06-43 =
//      RisingLowsIntoFlatCeiling). THIS OR IS WHY THE TEMPLATE IS ONE FILE.
//   3. NO SUSTAINED PRIOR PENETRATION (G06-44 = NoSustainedPriorPenetration),
//      measured against THE SAME level part 1 counted touches on.
//   4. AN ENTRY TRIGGER inside the base, selected by triggerMode.
//
// SIDE ASSIGNMENT IS THE REASON PANES CANNOT ASSEMBLE THIS. The long side needs
// LevelTouchCount's RESISTANCE reading, which lives on that rule's SHORT side,
// and it needs BreakoutBase's and RisingLowsIntoFlatCeiling's LONG sides -- the
// same obstruction AscendingTriangleIntoLevel carries. A placement cannot
// borrow the other side's condition (the DistanceAboveRecentLow /
// PullbackFromRecentHigh precedent), so no pane-AND reaches this.
//
// THE LEVEL IS SHARED STATE, not three separate levels: the most recent
// confirmed swing pivot high (on the support side, low) inside the rangeBars
// window is what parts 1 and 3 both read, which is what the book means by "the
// level". The base's own floor and ceiling -- the shifted baseBars window
// extremes -- are what the triggers read. That sharing is the other reason this
// is one file.
//
// TRIGGERMODE, NOT AN OR, FOR THE THREE TRIGGERS. The book gives three (G06-45
// bid near the base's floor, G06-46 enter on a spring through it, G06-47 "just
// get the position on, anywhere in the range"), but as BAR TESTS they are
// NESTED, not alternatives: every spring bar (a bar trading below the floor
// by minPenetrationAtr ATRs) also satisfies the floor-touch test (a bar trading
// at or below the floor plus tolAtr ATRs), and G06-47 has no bar test at all, so an OR of
// the three collapses to "true". They differ only in ORDER TYPE -- a resting
// limit bid, a stop or market order on the spring's close, a market order --
// which the engine does not model. So they are SELECTED: triggerMode 0 enters
// on the base alone (G06-47), 1 bids at the base floor (G06-45), 2 or more
// waits for the spring (G06-46). Default 1, the book's first. The
// requirePriorBreakout input on FinalFlagReversalBreakoutEntry is the precedent
// for a structural input on a named setup.
//
// INPUTS ARE SHARED WHERE THE PARTS SHARE A WINDOW, which is what keeps the
// count at 14 rather than 22: baseBars is the tight base, the recent half of
// the rising-lows test and the trigger window at once; rangeBars is the larger
// range, the pivot search window and the touch-count window; one tolAtr is
// every level tolerance (decision 7's 0.1 to 0.25 band) and one atrLength every
// ATR read. Grimes's own values that were dropped in favour of a shared input
// are recorded in the catalog row.
//
// MEASURED SWING PIVOT (decision 14, EL_Swing_Probe.txt): Occurrence 1 is the
// most recent CONFIRMED pivot, lag exactly strength bars, rangeBars is EL's
// Length with offsets 0..rangeBars-1 and a neighbourhood test reaching strength
// bars past the window, and an empty window returns the -1 SENTINEL. THE
// SENTINEL IS GUARDED ON THE BAR INDEX (SwingHighBar / SwingLowBar <> -1),
// never on the returned price: a back-adjusted series can print -1.00.
//
// THE TWO For LOOPS ARE GUARDED at >= 1. EasyLanguage's For counts DOWN when
// the end value is below the start, so `for kk = 1 to 0` would execute at
// kk = 1 and kk = 0 and read the CURRENT bar into windows meant to exclude it,
// where the C++ for-loop would not run at all.
//
// SHIFT TEST: every comparison is a price against a price from the same series
// or against an ATR multiple; touches and run lengths are COUNTS.
//
// WFSafe_AvgTrueRange is MultiWalk's ROLLING accumulator, a different function
// from the built-in it resembles; the C++ twin reproduces that form.
//
// Deepest bar touched:
// max(rangeBars - 1 + strength, 2 * baseBars - 1, preLookback, atrLength + 1).
//
// THE C++ TWIN WRITES OUT BY HAND what the EL twin says with built-ins. Highest/Lowest
// bottom out in Extremes(), EL SOURCE, so each window is a scan SEEDED ON THE
// WINDOW'S FIRST BAR and continued with a STRICT comparison under the EL
// relational tolerance (el_gt / el_lt), which is what keeps a tie on the
// EARLIEST bar -- Breakout is the reference. The swing-pivot scan is
// LevelTouchCount's inlined Occurrence-1 form: MIXED tie sense -- STRICTLY
// beyond the strength NEWER bars (the pk < pj loop, since bars-ago indexing
// makes the smaller index the newer bar) and AT OR BEYOND the strength OLDER
// bars -- with the confirmation lag built in by starting the candidate scan at
// pj = strength. Existence is a BOOL set where the scan finds the pivot, never
// a price compared with -1.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry rather than a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement; loop counters and
// per-candidate flags are block-scoped inside their loops.
if (atrLenState != (int)atrLength) {
    atrSum = 0.0;
    for (int aa = 0; aa < (int)atrLength; ++aa) {
        hiV = high[aa] > close[aa + 1] ? high[aa] : close[aa + 1];
        loV = low[aa] < close[aa + 1] ? low[aa] : close[aa + 1];
        atrSum += hiV - loV;
    }
    atrLenState = (int)atrLength;
} else {
    outIdx = (int)atrLength;
    hiV = high[0] > close[1] ? high[0] : close[1];
    loV = low[0] < close[1] ? low[0] : close[1];
    hiO = high[outIdx] > close[outIdx + 1] ? high[outIdx] : close[outIdx + 1];
    loO = low[outIdx] < close[outIdx + 1] ? low[outIdx] : close[outIdx + 1];
    atrSum += hiV - loV;
    atrSum -= hiO - loO;
}
atrValue = atrSum / (int)atrLength;
lvlHi = 0.0;
haveHi = false;
for (int pj = (int)strength; pj <= (int)rangeBars - 1; ++pj) {
    bool pok = true;
    for (int pk = pj - (int)strength; pk < pj; ++pk) {
        if (!el_gt(high[pj], high[pk])) { pok = false; break; }
    }
    if (pok) {
        for (int pk = pj + 1; pk <= pj + (int)strength; ++pk) {
            if (!el_ge(high[pj], high[pk])) { pok = false; break; }
        }
    }
    if (pok) { lvlHi = high[pj]; haveHi = true; break; }
}
lvlLo = 0.0;
haveLo = false;
for (int pj = (int)strength; pj <= (int)rangeBars - 1; ++pj) {
    bool pok = true;
    for (int pk = pj - (int)strength; pk < pj; ++pk) {
        if (!el_lt(low[pj], low[pk])) { pok = false; break; }
    }
    if (pok) {
        for (int pk = pj + 1; pk <= pj + (int)strength; ++pk) {
            if (!el_le(low[pj], low[pk])) { pok = false; break; }
        }
    }
    if (pok) { lvlLo = low[pj]; haveLo = true; break; }
}
touchHi = 0;
touchLo = 0;
for (int kk = 1; kk <= (int)rangeBars; ++kk) {
    if (el_ge(high[kk], lvlHi - tolAtr * atrValue) && el_lt(close[kk], lvlHi + tolAtr * atrValue)) {
        touchHi = touchHi + 1;
    }
    if (el_le(low[kk], lvlLo + tolAtr * atrValue) && el_gt(close[kk], lvlLo - tolAtr * atrValue)) {
        touchLo = touchLo + 1;
    }
}
tightHigh = high[0];
tightLow = low[0];
for (int i = 1; i <= (int)baseBars - 1; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], tightHigh)) tightHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], tightLow)) tightLow = low[i];
}
rangeHigh = high[0];
rangeLow = low[0];
for (int i = 1; i <= (int)rangeBars - 1; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], rangeHigh)) rangeHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], rangeLow)) rangeLow = low[i];
}
priHi = high[(int)baseBars];
priLo = low[(int)baseBars];
for (int i = (int)baseBars + 1; i <= 2 * (int)baseBars - 1; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], priHi)) priHi = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], priLo)) priLo = low[i];
}
baseFloor = low[1];
baseCeil = high[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)baseBars; ++i) {
    if (el_lt(low[i], baseFloor)) baseFloor = low[i];
    if (el_gt(high[i], baseCeil)) baseCeil = high[i];
}
runHi = 0;
runLo = 0;
maxRunHi = 0;
maxRunLo = 0;
for (int kk = 1; kk <= (int)preLookback; ++kk) {
    if (el_gt(close[kk], lvlHi + tolAtr * atrValue)) {
        runHi = runHi + 1;
        if (runHi > maxRunHi) maxRunHi = runHi;
    } else {
        runHi = 0;
    }
    if (el_lt(close[kk], lvlLo - tolAtr * atrValue)) {
        runLo = runLo + 1;
        if (runLo > maxRunLo) maxRunLo = runLo;
    } else {
        runLo = 0;
    }
}
baseUp = el_le(tightHigh - tightLow, tightFraction * (rangeHigh - rangeLow))
    && el_le(rangeHigh - tightHigh, proximityFraction * (rangeHigh - rangeLow));
baseDn = el_le(tightHigh - tightLow, tightFraction * (rangeHigh - rangeLow))
    && el_le(tightLow - rangeLow, proximityFraction * (rangeHigh - rangeLow));
riseUp = el_gt(tightLow, priLo + riseAtr * atrValue)
    && el_le(std::fabs(tightHigh - priHi), tolAtr * atrValue);
riseDn = el_lt(tightHigh, priHi - riseAtr * atrValue)
    && el_le(std::fabs(tightLow - priLo), tolAtr * atrValue);
trigUp = false;
trigDn = false;
if (el_le(triggerMode, 0.0)) {
    trigUp = true;
    trigDn = true;
}
if (el_gt(triggerMode, 0.0) && el_lt(triggerMode, 2.0)) {
    trigUp = el_le(low[0], baseFloor + tolAtr * atrValue);
    trigDn = el_ge(high[0], baseCeil - tolAtr * atrValue);
}
if (el_ge(triggerMode, 2.0)) {
    trigUp = el_lt(low[0], baseFloor - minPenetrationAtr * atrValue) && el_ge(close[0], baseFloor);
    trigDn = el_gt(high[0], baseCeil + minPenetrationAtr * atrValue) && el_le(close[0], baseCeil);
}
